# Scope statement and WBS — Planning
#
# Process Groups: A Practice Guide, 5.3 Define Scope and 5.4 Create WBS.

screen: scope
title: Scope and WBS
phase: Planning
process: 5.3 Define Scope / 5.4 Create WBS
order: 40
requires: charter,requirements
teaches: |
  How to describe the deliverable precisely enough that everyone means the
  same thing by it, and how to cut it into pieces small enough to estimate. By
  the end you should be able to explain the 100% rule and why a WBS decomposes
  deliverables rather than activities.
before: |
  Requirements, because the scope statement is what satisfies them. Writing a
  WBS before you know what the thing has to do produces a tidy breakdown of the
  wrong product.
after: |
  The schedule and the estimates. Work packages are what get sequenced, dated
  and assigned; nothing else in the plan attaches to anything smaller.
intro: |
  The scope statement describes the deliverable in enough detail that everyone
  means the same thing by it. The work breakdown structure then cuts that scope
  into pieces small enough to estimate, assign and check off.

  The WBS decomposes deliverables, not activities. Each item is a noun: a thing
  that will exist when the work is done.
source: |
  Process Groups: A Practice Guide (PMI, 2022), processes 5.3 Define Scope (output: the project scope
  statement) and 5.4 Create WBS (outputs: the WBS and the WBS dictionary), both
  in the Planning process group.

field product_scope
  prompt: Describe the deliverable in enough detail that two people would build the same thing.
  check words>=15: Too vague to build against. 'Done' will be judged against each person's mental picture.
  label: Product scope description
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: Project scope statement contents: product scope description.
  why: |
    It describes the characteristics of the thing being produced, elaborated
    from the charter as more becomes known. The charter says what; this says
    what it is like.
  if_blank: |
    "Done" is judged against each person's mental picture of the product.
  example: |
    A single-window desktop application with one screen per lifecycle artifact,
    a help panel that explains every field, and plain-text project records.

field acceptance_criteria
  prompt: What has to be true before the sponsor accepts it?
  check words>=10: These are the conditions you will be held to at validation. Vague ones become an argument on the last day.
  label: Deliverable acceptance criteria
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: Project scope statement contents: acceptance criteria.
  why: |
    These are the conditions the deliverable must meet before the sponsor
    accepts it. They are the checklist used in 5.5 Validate Scope, when
    deliverables are formally accepted.
  if_blank: |
    Acceptance becomes a matter of mood on the day.
  example: |
    Installs per-user, launches to the project list, and every field's help
    panel names its source in the standard.

field exclusions
  prompt: What is explicitly out of scope?
  check filled: Without exclusions, scope creep has nothing to run into.
  label: Exclusions
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    Project scope statement contents: project exclusions. Formalizes the
    charter's boundaries.
  why: |
    Explicitly stating what is out of scope manages stakeholder expectations
    and prevents the slow accumulation of "while you're in there" work.
  if_blank: |
    Scope creep has nothing to run into.
  example: |
    No resource leveling. No critical path calculation. No multi-user sync.

field wbs
  prompt: Break the deliverable into pieces small enough to estimate. What are they?
  check rows>=4: A breakdown with fewer than four items has not decomposed anything yet.
  check column:item: Every row needs a name. A WBS item is a noun: a thing that will exist when the work is done.
  label: Work breakdown structure
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: tree
  outline_column: code
  title_column: item
  tone_column: status
  source: |
    5.4 Create WBS. The WBS is a hierarchical decomposition of the total scope
    of work to be carried out by the project team. The lowest level is the work
    package.
  why: |
    Decomposition turns a deliverable that is too big to estimate into pieces
    that are not. The rule of thumb is the 100% rule: the children of any node
    add up to all of the parent, no more and no less.

    Work packages are where estimates and assignments attach. If you cannot
    estimate an item, it is not decomposed far enough; if you are tracking
    hours on it, it is decomposed too far.
  if_blank: |
    Estimates are made against a whole deliverable at once, which is the least
    accurate way to make them, and progress can only be described as a
    percentage somebody felt.
  example: |
    1.2 | Charter screen | Deliverable | Delivery team | In progress
  column code
    label: WBS code
    type: line
    width: 1
  column item
    label: Item
    type: line
    width: 5
  column kind
    label: Level
    type: choice
    options: Deliverable=accent, Work package=neutral, Milestone=warn
    width: 2
  column owner
    label: Owner
    type: line
    width: 2
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Not started=faint, In progress=accent, Done=ok, Blocked=danger
    width: 2
  column notes
    label: WBS dictionary note
    type: text
    width: 4
